scripts/dtc: drop the redundant yylloc definition

The bundled dtc defines YYLTYPE yylloc twice: once in the lexer and once in the
bison-generated parser. Both are tentative definitions, so a compiler defaulting
to -fcommon merges them and the host tool links. gcc 10 made -fno-common the
default, and the two become competing definitions:

  scripts/dtc/dtc-parser.tab.o:(.bss+0x10): multiple definition of `yylloc';
  scripts/dtc/dtc-lexer.lex.o:(.bss+0x0): first defined here

The parser's definition is the one the generated code expects, so the lexer's is
what goes. This is upstream commit e5e2cbf6ed76 ("scripts/dtc: Remove redundant
YYLOC global declaration"), which landed in 5.6 and was never backported this
far.

build-sdk.sh works around the same thing by passing -fcommon in HOSTCFLAGS.
That is left alone: it still builds, this patch simply means it no longer has to.

Both the flex source and the pre-generated lexer are changed. The kernel builds
the _shipped copy unless flex regenerates it, so patching only one of the two
leaves the failure in place depending on which the build picks.

--- a/sigmastar/kernel/4.9.84/scripts/dtc/dtc-lexer.l
+++ b/sigmastar/kernel/4.9.84/scripts/dtc/dtc-lexer.l
@@ -38,7 +38,6 @@
 #include "srcpos.h"
 #include "dtc-parser.tab.h"
 
-YYLTYPE yylloc;
 extern bool treesource_error;
 
 /* CAUTION: this will stop working if we ever use yyless() or yyunput() */
--- a/sigmastar/kernel/4.9.84/scripts/dtc/dtc-lexer.lex.c_shipped
+++ b/sigmastar/kernel/4.9.84/scripts/dtc/dtc-lexer.lex.c_shipped
@@ -637,7 +637,6 @@
 #include "srcpos.h"
 #include "dtc-parser.tab.h"
 
-YYLTYPE yylloc;
 extern bool treesource_error;
 
 /* CAUTION: this will stop working if we ever use yyless() or yyunput() */
